---
title: Corpus map — variants
teaser: "Four ways of drawing the same 100 entries and their links, for comparison."
layout: layouts/base.njk
permalink: /stats/map-variants/
noindex: true
eleventyExcludeFromCollections: true
---
<section>
  <h1 class="kh-font-headline kh-font-headline--display kh-text-heading--1">{{title}}</h1>

  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>A scratch page for comparison, not for keeping.</strong> Same data
    throughout: {{ semanticMap.nodes | length }} entries, {{ semanticMap.edges | length }}
    links at similarity ≥ {{ semanticMap.edgeMin }}. Only the drawing changes.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="a" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">A</span>Projection <span class="kh-meta">currently live</span></h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">position = similarity</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p>Position comes from classical MDS, so distance on the page approximates
    distance in meaning. Edges are drawn on top but are not what places the
    points. The honest one: if two dots are close, they really are related.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-map-scroll">{% semanticMap semanticMap %}</div>

  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="b" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">B</span>Force-directed</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">position = whatever the simulation settled on</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p>The same graph relaxed with a spring simulation, seeded on a circle and run
    with a fixed number of steps so it is deterministic. Point size shows how
    many links an entry has. It looks the most like a network, and it is the
    least trustworthy: position encodes nothing, so two dots can end up adjacent
    purely because the layout had nowhere else to put them. It also took four
    rounds of parameter tuning to stop it collapsing into a blob or pinning
    nodes to the walls, which is itself an argument against it.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-map-scroll">{% semanticForce semanticMap %}</div>

  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="c" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">C</span>Adjacency matrix</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">rows grouped by topic</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p>Every entry is a row and a column, grouped by primary topic. A filled cell
    means that pair is linked, darker where the similarity is higher. A matrix
    cannot become a hairball: density is read directly, blocks on the diagonal
    are clusters, and off-diagonal marks are the cross-topic pairings that a
    node-link diagram tends to bury.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-map-scroll">{% semanticMatrix semanticMap %}</div>

  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="d" class="kh-text-heading--2"><span class="kh-section-number">D</span>Arc diagram</h2>
    <span class="kh-meta">one line, links as arcs</span>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p>Entries on a single line in topic order, each link an arc. Compact enough
    to sit in a strip rather than needing a whole panel, and the span of an arc
    shows at a glance whether a link stays inside a topic or reaches across the
    corpus.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-map-scroll">{% semanticArc semanticMap %}</div>

  <div class="kh-section-head kh-u-margin__top--1200">
    <h2 id="verdict" class="kh-text-heading--2">Where I land</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="kh-content">
    <p><strong>A for the stats page, C if one had to be picked for insight.</strong>
    A is the only one where position carries meaning, which is what a "map"
    implies. C is the most informative but reads as an instrument rather than an
    illustration. B is the most conventional and the least honest. D is the most
    compact and would suit a sidebar or a strip better than a feature.</p>
  </div>
</section>
